  "result": "**Analogy: texting a roommate who is at home while you are in class**\n\n- **Overview**\n  - You can only send text messages. You cannot see the kitchen.\n  - Your roommate is the only one who can open the fridge.\n\n- **Worked example**\n  1. You text: \"Check the fridge: is there milk?\" This is the tool call. It is a written request, not an action.\n  2. Your roommate opens the fridge. This is the program running the tool.\n  3. They text back: \"Half a carton.\" This is the tool result.\n  4. You read it and decide: \"Then I'll skip the store.\" This is the next step, or the final answer.\n\n- **Mapping to the makerspace assistant**\n  - You are the model. You choose which tool to ask for, and you write the arguments.\n  - The roommate is your Python program. It runs the tool, for example the calendar lookup.\n  - The fridge is the booking calendar.\n  - Each text you send after reading a reply is a fresh model call.\n\n- **Where it breaks down**\n  - Your roommate remembers earlier messages. The model does not, so the program must resend the whole conversation each time.\n\n- **Check yourself**\n  - If the model asks for \"book the laser cutter Friday\", has anything been booked yet? Explain your reasoning in two sentences, and I will rate it 1-10.",
